f the things that we see  is that what happens in that time when  people are waiting is incredibly  important and you can imagine for  children one of the most important  things is that we actually take that  opportunity to invest in their education  at 13 million children have had their  education destroyed by the conflict in  Syria and in North Africa and when they  arrived here they start to say I want to  use education to put the pieces of my  life back together and we have an  opportunity to enable them to do that  but of course as we know a lot of strain  on school places and things like that  beginning of a school year at this very  moment  it's a question of resourcing as well  another thing you'll be wanting to hear  from the Prime Minister that local  authorities will want to hear about  resources going into things like  education absolutely housing and medical  help absolutely and I think this is the  time when the statutory sector the  government and the voluntary sector  really need to be able to work together  to provide the resources that people  need so taking the example of education  that you mentioned we work with a large  number of volunteer mentors who will  support teachers support social workers  to help individual children progress in  their education and that's the type of  support that we could see set up across  a number of different sectors whether  it's health whether it's education and  to respond to this need what do you  think about the the numbers involved  here you know if it's set 10,000 even if  its twenty thousand there are millions  of people just in Syria alone on our  outside Syria who've been affected there  are many more from other countries  they're at rest sudan just it just to  name a few do you think Britain should  be doing more you're right this is  absolutely a global crisis and our  attention right now is on Syria but it's  easy to forget some of the longer crises  like what's happening in the Democratic  Republic of Congo for example and I  think what we need is a three-pronged  approach that will look at durable  solutions in the long term so looking at  what can be done to build peace in those  regions what can be done to increase  resources in the intermediate host  countries so places like Jordan Lebanon  Turkey that are taking the bulk of the  refugees but then also there's immediate  needs on our doorstep as well and we've  got a responsibility to look I mean when  they do come into the European Union it  doesn't look as if Britain is doing as  much all it is an absolute fact not just  about looks is not doing as much as  places like Germany or Sweden the  response in Germany has been absolutely  phenomenal over the last couple of weeks  and you're right we can't have a  situation where the burden of this  crisis was entirely on just a couple of  European countries we need a shared  response and we need the UK to get  involved in that okay very good to talk  to Catherine glad well thank you very  much
 

